gCK5qsIW7x2qEa07ocaXIewQ5Eo6gi8FkH0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Fsxb2relnOlJRwqIx1LBC5Y9dCUzKA4aZsEhX6N1jPIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ln4yrs7DuPNjrpSBUPOOOjkocvwdIkV4x4FupA3rbhcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;UYcFamz7BZukmZA0xb7Z-PegA-gJdYO_JGHLLXS7HpMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;nYTdO5WiyziaIy7ccpq6ft6cnmpnfK5P3eCIt4eS868ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$2936c380-5900-4708-88c9-7a5c0ade2590ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jO1PD8k3La4Pp6Thw3rsWH6_72g4vAsJYOUzlixrqJgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky>After seven years during which the original GitHub repository sat frozen at its last commit, NextBSD has returned to active developmentâ€”under new management, with a new repository separate from the original, and the same founding purpose: porting Apple's open-source Darwin code into the FreeBSD ecosystem. [The Register's FOSS desk reported the revival on July 18, 2026.](https://www.theregister.com/os-platforms/2026/07/18/nextbsd-returns-to-dollop-apple-source-on-freebsd/5273788)

NextBSD was started in 2015 by Jordan Hubbard, a co-founder of FreeBSD. The plan from the beginning was to port components of Apple's Darwin OS to FreeBSD. Darwin is the Unix foundation on which macOS and Apple's other operating systems are built; it is open source, with its code available directly from GitHub. Bridging that codebase into FreeBSD required sustained porting effortâ€”work that exists only as long as someone is actively doing it.

## NextBSD Returns: Apple Darwin Source Porting Under New FreeBSD Stewardship

The developer who revived the project is Joe Maloney, known on GitHub as pkgdemon. Maloney is already recognized in BSD circles: he built the Gershwin desktop environment for GhostBSD, which The Register previously covered. Shortly after that coverage, Maloney approached the NextBSD maintainers and asked if he could take over the project. He had a relatively minor role in the originalâ€”his commits are visible in the project's historyâ€”and now leads the revived effort.

The new GitHub repository is distinct from the archived original, which remains accessible with its history intact. The revived project also includes a project history page that gives credit where it's due, as The Register describes it.

## What Apple Source Integration on FreeBSD Actually Requires

NextBSD's specific work is integrating Apple's open-source Darwin components into FreeBSD. This does not happen automatically: it requires deliberate porting effort each time Apple releases relevant Darwin code. Darwin's being open source makes the code available, but availability is not the same as accessibility across ecosystems. The porting work is what keeps Darwin components circulating in FreeBSD rather than remaining only where Apple's own infrastructure uses them.

The seven-year gap in the original repository's commit history is the visible record of what this work looks like when its maintainer steps back. The project did not close; the last commits simply stopped accumulating, and the gap persisted.

## What the Revival Picks Up

Two artifacts from the original project document what Maloney is now taking on. First, some of NextBSD's initial goals are explained in a presentation referenced in [The Register's coverage](https://www.theregister.com/os-platforms/2026/07/18/nextbsd-returns-to-dollop-apple-source-on-freebsd/5273788) of the revival, giving a record of the scope that Hubbard set out when he founded the project in 2015. Second, Maloney's own commit list in the original repository is publicly accessibleâ€”The Register notes that readers can see it directlyâ€”and it shows a minor contribution record rather than a core-developer one. That minor record is nonetheless the history that connected Maloney to the project before he approached its maintainers about taking over.

The Register's FOSS desk, which had previously covered Maloney's Gershwin desktop work on GhostBSD, described NextBSD as one of the most interesting BSD variants of the 2010s and said it was intrigued by the revival. That editorial attention marks the project as one with a specific kind of open-source press following: not a mainstream distribution, but a technically significant effort that attracted interest when it was active and is attracting interest again now that it has resumed. Maloney's new repository is distinct from the original, but the original remains accessible, and the new project history page gives credit where it's due.

Whether the revived NextBSD builds a contributor base broad enough to reduce its reliance on any single person's continued involvement is a question the new repository's commit history has only begun to answer.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QiS0jSQlPICOIoJkT6KgXthQu0Q0anCtNUlUf0HBlJoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;3LwRhkgQlJtW6cMenJ6xTVMr2pgcSu0DdVd-ldvKd0wƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;GRUQFcok4O5L6rcTwM97UCojsRSWtLyRgFrqqxXh4foƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AUrMbp60MCB1l-mKCE6skFkL_JMu3nXrx02Q5Er-GpUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PGa0v0rNKVRnqw4Bqy9vuvKgwSekrdFlZ04aM72pSyUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fRdFjszxg3O0kwTkFKofD6OhOmbq92yLZFCnd60c5SEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg155.414ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;p8eMj7phDoYpHxYQNVt1fHsetYYhCbwAyKYyOzDvGNEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bSWTx-_NOCckUvMdcZmjHuB-iAMPU7jF6_hv5XIDHnwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;RXVzI2OGhe2eTC1Pu4KvbZJIqxa7ZK0CbLT_iKvW018ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;RsjixwJkwneOmJxqsbYRdDRMgRGrM3MT992FEq7D1rUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxexchange-20260718-082655-5ef8aaƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;G6EX