SUMMARY:

The manuscript fails a single deterministic gate: body word count is 527 against a minimum of 600. No other deterministic gates are recorded as failing in the current standards read. Factual fidelity against the one on-topic source is largely sound. The shortfall is not the result of a thin source packet â€” The Register summary contains enough distinct reported facts to sustain a 600-word piece â€” but of a draft that substitutes generic analytical framing in its third section for a closer reading of the available reporting. A bounded repair (adding approximately 73 words of reported material, not padding) should clear the length gate.

---

FINDINGS:

The first two body sections read well. The lede is specific and faithful: it names the event concretely, attributes it to The Register's July 18 reporting, and correctly identifies the seven-year dormancy of the original repository. The second section develops the key figures and actions â€” Hubbard's 2015 founding, Darwin's relationship to macOS, Maloney's Gershwin background, and the transfer of stewardship â€” all of which are supported by the source summary.

The third section, "FreeBSD Infrastructure Fragility and the Seven-Year Gap in Apple Source Coverage," is the problem area, and it is the reason the word count is inadequate rather than merely unlucky. The section does not add reported facts; it restates what has already been said (seven-year gap, porting work, individual contributor dependency) through an analytical frame that the source does not license with specifics. The genre contract requires each section to "advance at least one supported claim rather than restating the frame." This section fails that standard, and because it fills space with inference rather than reporting, the overall count cannot reach even 600 words from the source material that was actually used.

The closing sentence â€” "Whether the revived NextBSD builds a contributor base broad enough to reduce dependency on any single person's continued involvement is a question the new repository's commit history has only begun to answer" â€” satisfies the requirement to end on an unresolved question facing the named actors, but it is more abstract than the source warrants. The Register summary mentions that the source article included a link to Maloney's commit history in the original project and that some initial goals were explained in a presentation; neither detail was used in the draft. Either could ground a more specific closing paragraph.

Two minor unsupported elaborations appear: "the maintainers agreed" (the source says he asked; agreement is implied by the revival but not stated), and "including the work Hubbard established when he founded the project in 2015" as a specification of what the new history page credits (the source says only that it gives credit where it's due, without naming Hubbard specifically in that context). Neither is materially misleading, but both should be tightened to stay within the source's actual statement.

The tweet field reproduces the article title verbatim with no emoji, which is below standard but is not a deterministic gate failure as reported.

---

CLAIM AUDIT:

Supported and correctly attributed: the seven-year gap in the original repository's commit history; the existence of a new, separate GitHub repository; the old repository remaining accessible; the new project history page; Jordan Hubbard's co-founding of FreeBSD and his 2015 creation of NextBSD; the plan to port Darwin components to FreeBSD; Darwin as the open-source Unix foundation of macOS and Apple's other OSes, with code available from GitHub; Joe Maloney's identity and GitHub handle; his authorship of GhostBSD's Gershwin desktop; The Register's prior coverage of Gershwin; Maloney's approach to the original maintainers to request a transfer; his minor role in the original project's commit history.

Minor inferences presented as fact: "the maintainers agreed" (implied, not stated), and the credit page specifically recognizing Hubbard's 2015 founding work (the source states credit is given "where it's due" without itemizing recipients).

No invented numbers, no flatly unsupported causal claims, no attribution laundering into the Koios voice.

